            <div xml:id="d866">

                <pb facs="0825" n="735" xml:id="pg735"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.00/809</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Sweden</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Morris</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Stockholm</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1917-12-13T17:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 13,
                                1917, 5 p.m.</hi></date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received December 11, 11.50
                        a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1175. Saw the Swedish Minister for Foreign Affairs who informed me that last week
                    the Finnish government had asked the Swedish Government as to their disposition
                    regarding recognizing Finland’s independence. The Swedish Minister for Foreign
                    Affairs yesterday telegraphed to the Swedish representatives in London, Paris,
                    and Berlin to ascertain what position England, France, and Germany,
                    respectively, will take regarding recognizing Finland’s independence. Swedish
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs asked me if I could ascertain from my Government in
                    an informal and unofficial way as to what attitude we would take regarding
                    recognizing Finland’s independence. I told him I would cable you ascertain this
                    information and acquaint him with my reply. In the course of the conversation
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs told me confidentially that
                        Orlovski, who has been appointed official
                    representative to Sweden of the Russian Bolshevik government, called upon the
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs at his house this morning.
                        Orlovski said that he felt quite sure that Bolshevik
                    government would have no hesitancy in recognizing Finland’s independence. In
                    this conversation the Swedish Minister for Foreign Affairs gave me to understand
                    [what] I cabled you in my 1159<anchor n="1" xml:id="d866-f1"/> that Sweden would
                    not recognize the government of Bolshevik in Russia at present. They will wait
                    to see how matters develop in Russia.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Morris</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
